timeline.body:
I don't want to bike shed too much here, thanks for iterating on this so far ❤️ 

I'm wondering here if we can simplify the logic here quite a bit (for e.g. it's a bit confusing to me that we have this condition here `if (currentStoredHealthMetadata != null` - as the `currentStoredHealthMetadata` could indeed be null - however, the `null` case it's handled on the previous branch of the if statement where we reset the health stuff)

What if we don't differentiate between a `reset` health metadata case and a regular `upsert` health metadata case (due to settings change) ? 
Also what if we drop the `readyToPublishAsMaster` flag? (it _seems_ that it just guards against the whole cluster being upgraded, however we have a designated `if` check for that - unless I'm missing something I think we could drop this flag altogether)

For e.g. this `updateOnClusterStateChange` method could look like so:

```
    private void updateOnClusterStateChange(ClusterChangedEvent event) {
        // Avoid scheduling updates to the taskQueue in case the cluster is recovering.
        if (event.state().blocks().hasGlobalBlock(GatewayService.STATE_NOT_RECOVERED_BLOCK)) {
            return;
        }
        final boolean prevIsMaster = this.isMaster;
        if (prevIsMaster != event.localNodeMaster()) {
            this.isMaster = event.localNodeMaster();
        }

        if (this.isMaster) {
            // Wait until every node in the cluster is upgraded to 8.5.0 or later
            if (event.state().nodesIfRecovered().getMinNodeVersion().onOrAfter(Version.V_8_5_0)) {
                var currentStoredHealthMetadata = HealthMetadata.getFromClusterState(event.state());
                if (currentStoredHealthMetadata == null || this.localHealthMetadata.equals(currentStoredHealthMetadata) == false) {
                    logger.debug("updating health metadata to {}", this.localHealthMetadata);
                    taskQueue.submitTask("health-metadata-update", () -> this.localHealthMetadata, null);
                }
            }
        }
    }
```

What do you think?

